WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.113
- This is StarTalk.

00:00:04.357 --> 00:00:09.160
We are going to talk about the science

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.030
that exists, or the absence of science

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.110
that doesn't exist.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.960
- Yeah, probably the latter
more so than the former.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.610
- In the Marvel Universe, specifically

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.040
a entire show of spoilers on...

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.840
- Marvel Endgame.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.200
- Endgame, Endgame.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.020
So if you haven't seen Endgame yet,

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.750
just shut off the program now.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.583
Now.

00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:32.416
- You should not be watching.

00:00:32.416 --> 00:00:33.880
- Now, okay.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.713
Now who's left?

00:00:35.891 --> 00:00:36.724
We got the tight--

00:00:36.724 --> 00:00:37.869
- All right, now that
those people are gone.

00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:39.132
(laughter)

00:00:39.132 --> 00:00:39.965
- Now that they're gone.

00:00:39.965 --> 00:00:40.798
- [Chuck] Now that they're gone.

00:00:40.798 --> 00:00:43.440
- All right, so let's
just talk about it, Chuck.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.850
- Yeah, so first of all--

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.460
- Don't get me started.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:47.417
Hold me back.

00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:48.250
- That should be the name of this.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.610
Don't Get Me Started.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:50.443
- Hold me back.

00:00:50.443 --> 00:00:55.443
- I saw the movie, of
course, and unfortunately

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.700
I didn't get to see it in IMAX.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:57.564
Did you actually go and see it in IMAX?

00:00:57.564 --> 00:01:00.370
- Yeah, I saw it in IMAX
three days before it opened,

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.230
and I Tweeted, do you remember my Tweet?

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.497
- I saw your Tweet, where you said,

00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:05.562
"I didn't know there were
so many Marvel characters."

00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:06.930
- I didn't know there
were so many Avengers.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:07.763
- Yes.

00:01:07.763 --> 00:01:09.320
- Maybe they're not all Avengers?

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.173
- That's a weird question.

00:01:11.173 --> 00:01:12.177
- There were just so many, and--

00:01:12.177 --> 00:01:14.760
- 'Cause no, 'cause the
Guarden of the Galaxys are not.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:15.593
Well no, 'cause they're Avengers.

00:01:15.593 --> 00:01:16.690
- Yes, they're Avengers.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.630
There are blue ones and
red ones and silver ones

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.900
and green ones and three of them

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.330
were named Chris, you know?

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.600
- That's funny.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.713
- What's up with that?

00:01:27.348 --> 00:01:30.840
So here's, let me just say something nice

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.660
about the Marvel Universe
before we get all up in it.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.493
- [Chuck] Okay.

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:35.550
- Okay.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.100
Of all the communities of
superheroes that exist,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:45.100
the Marvel superheroes, more than not,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.150
derive from some kind of
science or scientific principle,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.770
if not the people
themselves are themselves

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:54.690
scientifically literate.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.420
So you look at Spiderman.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.885
He's really Spiderboy, right?

00:01:58.885 --> 00:01:59.793
- Spiderboy.

00:01:59.793 --> 00:02:00.626
Spiderkid.

00:02:00.626 --> 00:02:01.459
- [Neil] Spiderkid.

00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:02.292
- Right.

00:02:02.292 --> 00:02:04.020
- He was in his science class, and he gets

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.960
bitten by a radioactive spider.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.670
So it's not that sitting with Miss Muffet

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:10.740
sitting on her tuffet.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.910
He's in science class when this happens.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.020
Dr. Banner is a medical
doctor, he becomes the Hulk.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.470
Ironman is Ironman, he's a total genius,

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:23.850
unlike Batman, not to
pit DC against Marvel,

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.260
but Batman is smart, but he's not the one

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.140
inventing the stuff
that makes him powerful.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.170
- Exactly.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.950
He's got his company.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.080
- He's got his whole company that's--

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:34.913
- Producing all of his stuff.

00:02:34.913 --> 00:02:36.580
- A military contracting company

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.560
with engineers in the
silo doing their work.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.350
So Ironman is doing all his own work.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.610
So I applaud Marvel.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.950
I don't know where the
science is in Hawkeye, okay?

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.520
He's got no powers.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.070
(laughter)

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.660
And I love watching Black Widow.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.920
Who doesn't love some good martial arts?

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.590
But we don't have to talk about the

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:01.721
science of that, 'cause that's.

00:03:01.721 --> 00:03:03.000
- Well, yeah, and they don't have powers.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.794
- [Neil] They don't have
powers, superhero powers.

00:03:04.794 --> 00:03:05.627
- They're just very good shots.

00:03:05.627 --> 00:03:06.900
- Yes. (laughs)

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:07.733
- Both of them.

00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:10.656
They'd be great for
like, maybe the biathlon.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:13.500
(laughter)

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.640
America once again
brings home Olympic gold

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.480
thanks to Hawkeye and
Black Widow, but yeah.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.650
- The ninja biathlon.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.411
- The ninja biathlon.

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:25.060
(laughter)

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:26.300
- So, all right.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.960
Oh, you have notes on this?

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.500
- No, I have questions.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.333
- Questions?

00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:31.792
- Actually, so what we did was--

00:03:31.792 --> 00:03:33.060
- So this is kinda like a Cosmic Queries.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.340
- It is kinda like a Cosmic Queries,

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.733
'cause we went online and we
solicited questions from--

00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:37.566
- You didn't tell me that.

00:03:37.566 --> 00:03:38.399
- From people who.

00:03:38.399 --> 00:03:39.232
Well you know.

00:03:39.232 --> 00:03:40.065
- [Neil] All right, all right, all right.

00:03:40.065 --> 00:03:43.367
- But people who wanted to
know some of the science

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:45.343
and your thoughts on it, they got to us.

00:03:45.343 --> 00:03:48.631
- Now, I didn't read the comic books.

00:03:48.631 --> 00:03:49.464
- [Chuck] Well you don't have to.

00:03:49.464 --> 00:03:50.297
It's not really about the comic books.

00:03:50.297 --> 00:03:51.350
- No, but they go to see these movies

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.781
and they complain that something
was in the comic that...

00:03:53.781 --> 00:03:55.270
It's like the people who read the novels

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:56.600
before they becomes the movies.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.840
They're the ones who really should never,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.605
not be allowed in the theater.

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:02.000
(laughter)

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.670
Because whatever I say about a movie

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.330
is not as obnoxious as a person
who read the novel first.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.500
- I gotta tell you you're right.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.830
So one of the things
that people don't know

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:14.730
about this is, in the
movie, in the comic books,

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:19.400
Hulk becomes Worldbreaker Hulk.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.800
And so in the movie Ragnarok, where Hulk

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.566
gets sent away to another planet.

00:04:25.566 --> 00:04:26.528
- This is Thor: Ragnarok.

00:04:26.528 --> 00:04:28.830
- This is Thor: Ragnarok.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:32.180
That's actually a storyline where Hulk

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.520
becomes this Worldbreaker
Hulk and he becomes

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.400
this kind of like, gladiator, right?

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.570
But then after that, he learns how to

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.360
separate Bruce Banner from the Hulk

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.260
while they are merged as
one, and then he becomes

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.670
kind of Bruce the Brulk.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.880
He's Bruce and Hulk at the same time.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:56.713
- Mm-hmm.

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:58.360
- And that's what you see in this movie.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.440
- Yes, in Endgame.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:02.740
- In Endgame, thank you.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:03.760
That he is...

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.890
So that kind of stuff you do see happening

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.710
in the movie, which I think is kinda cool,

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:08.706
and that should make--

00:05:08.706 --> 00:05:09.893
- Well, this is connective
tissue between storylines.

00:05:09.893 --> 00:05:11.903
- Between all the storylines, and that's

00:05:11.903 --> 00:05:13.750
what they did really well.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.880
- So Marvel has upheld the
storytelling end of this.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.420
- Yeah, and I think they did a great job

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:18.460
with the storytelling, and I think

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.940
Marvel stories are better
than DC stories period.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.800
I don't know if they
just hire better writers.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:23.633
- Blood drawn.

00:05:23.633 --> 00:05:25.350
- I'm sorry, I mean, I don't.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.130
If you're a DC person, good for you,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.000
but I think all their storylines in Marvel

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.610
are just so much more
captivating and compelling.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.670
The DC storylines tend to kind of have

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:42.270
a more magical, mystical kind
of crap stuff attached to it.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.770
I don't know, that's my
opinion, that means nothing.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.280
That's how I feel, but--

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.110
- Wait, wait, wait, excuse me.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.210
You telling me the powers
that Wonder Woman wields

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.410
are not differently mysterious and magical

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.893
from those of Captain Marvel?

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:05.386
- Yes, because Diana is a
god, goddess, basically.

00:06:05.386 --> 00:06:07.240
- [Neil] Yes.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.180
- And what's that mean?

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.180
I mean, that's such an easy thing to.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.233
Well, the only person
who's like that is Thor.

00:06:13.233 --> 00:06:14.066
- Yes, okay.

00:06:14.066 --> 00:06:16.600
- Thor is a god, okay, we get it, but.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:17.720
- So therefore, he does god stuff.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:18.660
- Yeah, he does god stuff.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:23.340
And when you create a
god, I mean, it's like,

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.997
okay, well yeah, I can do that, I'm a god,

00:06:25.997 --> 00:06:28.020
and to me, it's kinda lazy.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.200
- Ooh.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.787
- I'm just saying, it's
a little lazy, it's like.

00:06:30.787 --> 00:06:33.060
- All right, I don't
have a rebuttal to that.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.360
- Yeah, it's like, okay, I'm a god.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.990
Yeah, I did that because I'm a god.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.040
It's like, okay, so that's
where all your powers

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:45.040
come from, the fact that you
are this deity, all right.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.930
And to me, I'm like, okay, whereas

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:53.460
although the science is not tight

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.500
or there's no real fidelity
to the science, at least--

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.333
- [Neil] In Marvel.

00:06:58.333 --> 00:06:59.295
- In Marvel.

00:06:59.295 --> 00:07:00.128
- [Neil] There's a premise.

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:00.961
- At least there's a premise.

00:07:00.961 --> 00:07:05.961
Like for instance, gamma
radiation creating the Hulk.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:09.380
Gamma radiation, from what
I have learned from you

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.161
and every other scientist
that has been on this show.

00:07:11.161 --> 00:07:14.300
- It will kill the host
essentially every time, yes.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.150
- There's no way that
you could ever experience

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:18.600
gamma radiation...

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:19.670
- And come out walking out saying, oh,

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.370
I'm just a little different today.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:22.766
- I feel great.

00:07:22.766 --> 00:07:24.600
(laughter)

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.070
God, it's so refreshing, that gamma ray.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.287
So anyway, that's the way I felt.

00:07:29.287 --> 00:07:30.380
- Gimme some question.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.000
- And we always start with Patreon.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:33.833
- [Neil] Do it.

00:07:33.833 --> 00:07:35.370
- And here we go.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.543
Frank Cane.

00:07:37.847 --> 00:07:39.667
"Which movie actually
represented time travel

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.557
"more consistently with modern theories,

00:07:42.557 --> 00:07:44.520
"Endgame or Back to the Future?"

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:45.750
Wow, they picked one of your favorite.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.530
- Yeah, I gotta go with, well.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:49.160
Maybe the stuff that happened in Endgame

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.760
that I didn't fully understand, but

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.700
Back to the Future, Back
to the Future tried to be

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:59.260
as consistent as it possibly
could at every turn.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.600
I'm talking about Back to the Future One,

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.583
not Two and Three.

00:08:01.583 --> 00:08:02.814
- Not Two and Three.

00:08:02.814 --> 00:08:03.688
- And so...

00:08:03.688 --> 00:08:05.473
- Marty, there's something
wrong with your kids!

00:08:05.473 --> 00:08:06.636
(laughter)

00:08:06.636 --> 00:08:07.469
Marty!

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:11.876
- What, do I become a jerk?

00:08:11.876 --> 00:08:12.784
No, it's your kids, Marty!

00:08:12.784 --> 00:08:13.837
- No, it's your kids, Marty!

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.113
- So...

00:08:16.113 --> 00:08:17.440
- [Chuck] Get me some garbage!

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.273
All right, sorry.

00:08:18.273 --> 00:08:20.890
- So, so...

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.840
I think, now what was the question?

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:23.673
Now you con...

00:08:23.673 --> 00:08:24.675
- I messed you up.

00:08:24.675 --> 00:08:27.400
Which movie represents time travel better?

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:28.233
- Time travel, right.

00:08:28.233 --> 00:08:29.200
- [Chuck] Endgame or Back to the Future.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.800
- Okay, so Endgame, if I'm to make that

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.850
internally consistent, it requires

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.540
a much more complex set of timelines

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.540
branching off of one another than

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:40.780
what happened in Back to the Future.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.020
All that happened in Back to the Future

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.910
is they created a single
alternative timeline.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:45.743
- Correct.

00:08:45.743 --> 00:08:47.540
- Which is kind of the timeline we wanted

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.630
there to have been all along, right?

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:52.473
And so it had very
satisfying elements to it.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:58.440
In Endgame, let me just think about

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.740
the multiple timelines here.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:03.070
Forget the fact that Captain America

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.600
decided to hang with his woman and not do

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.900
all the saving that he
did in the other movies

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:11.010
as Captain America in present day, okay?

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.900
There's still the present
day element of this

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:17.500
that now is lost to some timeline

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.360
that doesn't now exist in the
timeline we are left with.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.830
He's sitting there as
an old man on a bench,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.180
and we're happy for
him 'cause he went back

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:26.383
to the true love of his life.

00:09:26.383 --> 00:09:27.950
- Found his woman.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:29.460
- You can't, we're not gonna--

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.010
- You can't hate on that.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:31.843
It's very hard.

00:09:31.843 --> 00:09:34.440
- But how about whatever crime he stopped

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.250
in the episodes he was in?

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:37.083
What of that?

00:09:37.083 --> 00:09:38.480
Well, that's just some other universe

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.660
that's unfolding that way at this point.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.960
Because you didn't change the timeline,

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.370
you branched the timeline.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.050
That's the modern concept of what happens

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:48.350
when you change time.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.190
And so, plus, I didn't like the fact

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:53.410
that they interacted with each other.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:56.750
He's admiring his own ass, okay?

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:58.010
(laughter)

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:00.070
- I can't really blame him for that.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.713
- Chuck! (laughs)

00:10:01.713 --> 00:10:03.460
- I mean, first of all,
he's got a fantastic ass.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:04.620
- He's admiring...

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.550
And by the way, when I saw that scene

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.940
where he's knocked down on the ground,

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.050
of course he's gonna get back up,

