00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:06.830
My people, my physics progenitors here,

00:48:06.830 --> 00:48:08.400
they're scratching their heads.

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:10.610
What does it mean to not be able to fully

00:48:10.610 --> 00:48:15.110
know the reality of an
object, of a particle?

00:48:15.110 --> 00:48:18.340
Heisenberg noted you
can't simultaneously know

00:48:18.340 --> 00:48:20.910
the momentum of a particle or its position

00:48:21.930 --> 00:48:24.180
precisely at the same time.

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:25.670
So Einstein's uncertainty principle means

00:48:25.670 --> 00:48:30.670
you can't know with accuracy
the position of a particle

00:48:32.150 --> 00:48:34.900
and its momentum, which
means its velocity,

00:48:34.900 --> 00:48:37.890
in which speed and direction its going.

00:48:37.890 --> 00:48:39.480
You can't know both simultaneously.

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:42.720
The more you know of one the
less you know of the other.

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:43.670
So if you know the velocity of something,

00:48:43.670 --> 00:48:45.300
you don't know where it is.

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:47.760
If you don't know where
it is, then you know

00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:49.900
exactly how fast it's going, even though

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:52.318
you don't know where it is. (laughs)

00:48:52.318 --> 00:48:53.760
